course: dictionary
lesson: 14616
title: Popularise: making things known and understood
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To popularise something means to make it liked or known by lots of people. If everyone in your class suddenly starts playing the same game, you could say the game was popularised.
explain: The word has a second meaning too. When someone explains a hard subject, like science or history, in simple words so ordinary people can enjoy it, they popularise it. «The TV presenter helped to popularise space science for young viewers.»
explain: Popularise comes from popular, which means liked by many people. Add -ise and it becomes an action: to make something popular. Check your meaning by swapping it in: «The film made skateboarding popular» means the same as «The film popularised skateboarding.»
fact: Popularise is a verb, so it shows an action: popularise, popularises, popularised, popularising.
fact: «The film popularised skateboarding.» means the film made skateboarding known and liked by many people.
fact: Popularise can also mean explaining a hard subject in a simple way so ordinary people can understand it.
fact: «Popularised» is often used in history and science, when an idea, a game or a food spread because someone promoted it.
fact: In American English the spelling is popularize, with a z.
exercise: «The film popularised skateboarding.» What does popularised mean here? | Invented it for the first time | Made it dangerous and hard to do | Stopped people from doing it | *Made it liked and known by many people
exercise: A science presenter explains black holes in simple words so children can enjoy them. What is she doing? | *Popularising science | Hiding the science from everyone | Making the science longer and harder | Breaking the science into pieces
exercise: Last year, a famous singer ___ the new dance, and now everyone at school knows it. | Popularly | Popularity | Popular | *Popularised
test: «The book helped to popularise chess across the whole school.» What does popularise mean in this sentence? | *Made chess liked and known by many people | Banned chess from the playground | Wrote a new set of rules for chess | Made chess harder to learn
test: Which sentence uses popularise to mean explaining a hard idea simply? | The teacher popularised the hard idea by hiding it in a box. | *The teacher popularised the hard idea by using pictures and simple words. | The teacher popularised the hard idea by shouting at the class. | The teacher popularised the hard idea by forgetting it.
exam: «The new app popularised reading among children who had never liked books.» What is the best meaning of popularised here? | Stopped children from reading | Forgot how to read | Made reading illegal | *Made reading liked by many children
